      "id": 1,
      "text": "Campti's name comes from a Natchitoches Indian chief known to French colonists as \"Le Roi Campti\".",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campti%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first written record of Campti appears in 1745 when French missionary Père Valentin visited the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campti%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Union Brigadier‑General Smith burned what remained of Campti during the war on April 14, 1864.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campti%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After the war, cotton and agriculture stayed mainstays, and cattle raising later entered the local economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campti%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Campti lies on the eastern bank of the Red River, surrounded by flat farmland.",
